<a href="#section-converting" id="section-converting" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Converting between Email, MimeMessage, EML and Outlook .msg{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Say your support app gets a forwarded <code>.eml</code>. Convert it to <code>Email</code> to read the subject, body and attachments.
        If the next step needs Jakarta Mail, ask for a <code>MimeMessage</code>. Add <code>outlook-module</code> to do the same with Outlook <code>.msg</code> files.</p>
      <p>Choose a source, set any options, then ask for the result you want. For example, supply receive keys to open
        <a href="security.html#section-reading-smime">S/MIME</a> or <a href="security.html#section-reading-openpgp">OpenPGP/MIME</a> content.
        The static <code>EmailConverter</code> methods are also available.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">Path emlPath = Path.of("received.eml");
Path msgPath = Path.of("received.msg");

// Choose the source first, then the format you need
byte[] emlBytes = mail.converter().fromEmail(yourEmail).toEmlBytes();
String emlText = mail.converter().fromEmail(yourEmail).toEml();
MimeMessage mimeMessage = mail.converter().fromEmail(yourEmail)
    .withSession(yourSession).toMimeMessage();
Email email = mail.converter().fromMimeMessage(yourMimeMessage).toEmail();

// Simple Java Mail closes the stream it opens for each Path
Email parsedEml = mail.converter().fromEml(emlPath).toEmail();
EmailPopulatingBuilder editableEml = mail.converter().fromEml(emlPath).toEmailBuilder();
MimeMessage parsedMimeMessage = mail.converter().fromEml(emlPath).toMimeMessage();

Email parsedMsg = mail.converter().fromOutlookMsg(msgPath).toEmail();
OutlookEmailConversionResult msgWithSourceData =
    mail.converter().fromOutlookMsg(msgPath).toEmailBuilderWithOutlookData();

</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Know when the file is read.</strong> The <code>to...</code> call reads the input. Start with a new source selection for each
          conversion, including after a failed read. A builder returned by an import uses the factory settings captured for that conversion.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose how to provide the file.</strong> Pass a <code>Path</code> or <code>File</code> and Simple Java Mail closes the stream it opens.
          The fluent API leaves streams you supply open. You can also pass a String for in-memory EML.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose when to read attachments.</strong> <code>withFetchAttachmentData(false)</code> defers ordinary attachment reads.
          Protection processing and delivery reports may read what they need. See the <a href="#received-conversion-details">received-mail details</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose how much to change.</strong> Direct MIME/EML routes skip the editable Email stage. When exporting an Email, use
          <code>withEmailGovernance(...)</code> to apply templates explicitly. To submit the original bytes, see <a href="#section-exact-eml">exact EML submission</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-source-inspection" id="section-source-inspection" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
    <h2>Inspect received content and processing problems</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>A support ticket arrives with two attachments named <code>invoice.pdf</code>. One opens and the other has a damaged signature.
        Read the usable parts through the ordinary <code>Email</code> getters. Then use <code>getProblems()</code> to find which part hit trouble and
        <code>getSourceData()</code> for the input evidence Simple Java Mail captured.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.converter().fromEml(emlBytes).toEmail();
email.getPlainText();
email.getAttachments();

EmailSourceData source = email.getSourceData();
List&lt;EmailSourceHeader&gt; headers = source.getHeaders();
List&lt;EmailSourceProperty&gt; properties = source.getProperties();
List&lt;EmailSourcePart&gt; parts = source.getParts();
List&lt;EmailSourceData&gt; derivedViews = source.getDerivedSources();

for (EmailProblem problem : email.getProblems()) {
    EmailSourceIdentity origin = problem.getOrigin();
    List&lt;EmailSourceElement&gt; affectedSources = problem.getSources();
    // Inspect the observation and its affected source entries.
}</code></pre>
      <h3>Find the part that needs attention</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Missing or unreadable values.</strong> An unreadable sender with no raw fallback is absent and an unreadable recipient list is empty.
          The Problem retains the failure and any source references. A missing or unavailable Subject can be <code>null</code>.
          A readable empty Subject stays empty, even when another view has a value. Use inspection if that distinction matters.</li>
        <li><strong>A recovered sender.</strong> If a malformed <code>Sender</code> supplies the fallback for a missing <code>From</code>, the recovered
          address remains readable and the Problem points to that header. <code>From</code> and <code>Reply-To</code> can share one observation.
          Successfully parsed and unused Sender headers need no recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>A problem inside an attachment.</strong> An embedded Outlook message converted to an EML attachment can contribute address Problems to its
          parent. Those Problems still point to the child's source. A malformed signed S/MIME attachment, or a file that your usable key cannot open, keeps its
          original attachment and a Problem linked to that occurrence. The parent keeps its own protection facts.</li>
        <li><strong>A read you need to stop.</strong> Interruption throws <code>CancellationException</code> with the original cause, even if optional
          S/MIME inspection is unavailable. A socket timeout by itself is separate. An unusable keystore or a direct low-level protection helper failure
          also fails the operation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Keep the evidence for the ticket</h3>
      <p>Source headers preserve spelling, raw values, repeats and available order, including fields excluded from outgoing composition.
        Visit a part's headers, properties and children for its own evidence. Native Outlook properties remain separate from derived views.
        Use <code>OutlookEmailConversionResult</code> for the specialized Outlook API.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">for (EmailSourcePart part : email.getSourceData().getParts()) {
    // Inspect part.getHeaders()/getProperties(), then visit part.getChildren().
    EmailSourceContent captured = part.getContent();
    if (captured != null) {
        long retainedBytes = captured.getSize();
        boolean complete = captured.isFullyCaptured();
    }
}</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Complete originals.</strong> Import retains complete supplied raw EML or MSG in its consumed format. Use
          <code>getInspection().getOriginalMessage()</code> for the file and <code>getOriginalAttachment(index)</code> for the matching attachment
          occurrence. An opened file can expose its usable bytes alongside its protected original. A supplied <code>MimeMessage</code> without captured
          raw bytes has no original file to return.</li>
        <li><strong>Captured payloads.</strong> Use <code>openStream()</code> or <code>readAllBytes()</code> to read captured content again. A partial
          capture gives you the prefix and its retained size. A null content value means no bytes were kept. Inspecting the capture leaves the external
          resource and the chosen eager or deferred read behavior alone.</li>
        <li><strong>What may use memory.</strong> Outlook RTF can be kept beside derived HTML, while a Base64 decode or filename recovery can reuse
          unchanged content. Raw originals and distinct decoded content may take extra memory. Copies can share those captures. Use import limits
          to bound import work and budget heap separately.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A delivery report can read its status part even with deferred attachment extraction. A failed read keeps the acquired prefix and a Problem.
        If only closing the stream failed, the complete capture remains. Ambiguous reports can reference several parts and a missing status part can
        reference its container. <code>problem.getSources()</code> exposes what was captured without a second read. Cancellation still propagates.
        See <a href="delivery-reports.html#imperfect-notifications">imperfect delivery notifications</a>.</p>

      <h3>Export a file and keep the diagnostics</h3>
      <p>If the ticket system stores EML, use a result method when importing an Outlook attachment and exporting it in one operation.
        Save the resulting bytes when available and record any Problems from that conversion.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">EmailConversionResult&lt;byte[]&gt; converted = mail.converter()
    .fromOutlookMsg(msgPath)
    .toEmlBytesResult();

if (converted.hasValue()) {
    Files.write(emlPath, converted.getValue());
}
List&lt;EmailProblem&gt; newProblems = converted.getProblems();
List&lt;EmailSourceData&gt; retainedSources = converted.getSources();</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>Usable output can come with recoverable Problems. When conversion can't produce an artifact, read <code>getFailure()</code>.
          Failed input reads and unrecognized MSG files cannot produce a successful empty artifact. Direct value methods throw that recognized failure,
          including static <code>EmailConverter</code> methods. Invalid use and unrelated application failures throw in either form.</li>
        <li>For a separate review step, import an inspection Email and decide later whether to export. Static MSG stream overloads close their input.
          Fluent conversion leaves your stream open.</li>
        <li>Problems belong to the operation that recorded them. Their source links and observed Message-ID survive copies, edits and supported
          serialization. Outgoing completion keeps history and unchanged associations. A replaced field has caller-supplied content with unknown
          incoming protection. Later reads and sends leave the original Problems alone.</li>
        <li>An empty Problems list gives you no proof that every part or signature was inspected. Expected transformations can retain sources without
          adding a Problem. Historic Problems leave outgoing validation and exact or protected byte requirements up to the send operation.
          Fresh composition starts with empty source data and Problems, and ordinary export keeps those records out of MIME and logs.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

<h2 id="section-receive-protected-content">Read protected content</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Suppose you accept signed reports from a partner and want to open a protected attachment. Supply the right receive keys on the conversion.
        Load <a href="/modules.html#smime-module"><code>smime-module</code></a> for S/MIME and
        <a href="/modules.html#openpgp-module"><code>openpgp-module</code></a> for OpenPGP/MIME.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">Pkcs12Config myKeyInfo = Pkcs12Config.builder()
    .pkcs12Store(Path.of("my_smime_keystore.pkcs12"))
    .storePassword("my_store_password")
    .keyAlias("my_key_alias")
    .keyPassword("my_key_password")
    .build();

OpenPgpReceiveConfig openPgpKeys = OpenPgpReceiveConfig.builder()
    .addVerificationKeyRing(senderPublicKeyRing)
    .addDecryptionKeyRing(recipientSecretKeyRing, recipientPassphrase)
    .build();

// Receive-side security options do not require placeholder null arguments
Email protectedEmail = mail.converter()
    .fromEml(emlPath)
    .withSmimeDecryption(myKeyInfo)
    .withOpenPgpReceiveConfig(openPgpKeys)
    .withSession(yourSession)
    .toEmail();

ProtectionInspection protection = protectedEmail.getInspection().getOpenPgp();
for (SignatureObservation signature : protection.getSignatures()) {
    SignatureVerification verification = signature.getStatus();
    String signingKeyFingerprint = signature.getSigningKeyFingerprint();
}
// Check the exact value your application consumes:
ProtectionCheck decision = documentRequirements.check(
    protectedEmail.getInspection().getAttachment(documentIndex));
</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Decide on the file you will use.</strong> Check the actual attachment or field against <code>ProtectionRequirements</code>.
          It checks approved signing keys, full signature coverage and decryption requirements using the recorded evidence without reading content,
          running cryptography or adding Problems. See the <a href="security.html#section-inspecting-received-protection">received-protection workflow</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the findings separate.</strong> Opening encrypted content, verifying its signature and approving the signer are separate decisions.
          The inspection records whether decryption was unavailable, deferred or failed. The selected value carries the coverage that was established.
          An unopened encrypted part leaves its inner signature and coverage unestablished.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Mailers and converters from one factory share its <a href="security.html#section-smime-key-cache">S/MIME key reuse capacity</a>.
        Separate factories keep separate key caches. That capacity counts key configurations. <a href="#conversion-import-limits">Import limits</a>
        apply to each message instead.</p>

      <h3>Check the keys before building</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>PKCS12.</strong> Supply the store, alias and both passwords. Use <code>""</code> or an empty <code>char[]</code> for an empty password.
          Missing choices throw <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> at build time. <code>getPkcs12StoreData()</code> can inspect an incomplete
          builder and returns the supplied bytes or null. Key and password getters return copies. The S/MIME operation validates the store,
          passwords and key when it uses them.</li>
        <li id="openpgp-receive-draft-validation"><strong>OpenPGP receive keys.</strong> <code>getVerificationKeyRings()</code> and
          <code>getDecryptionKeyRings()</code> return editable copies. Use <code>clearVerificationKeyRings()</code> or <code>clearDecryptionKeyRings()</code>
          to remove a set. <code>clearPassphraseProvider()</code> restores the passphrases supplied with the decryption keys. Required nulls fail in
          setters and empty key bytes fail at <code>build()</code>. Inspecting a builder skips the provider and cryptography. Built configurations and
          inspection results retain their own snapshots.</li>
        <li><strong>Outgoing OpenPGP choices.</strong> Signing and encryption builders expose their keys and algorithms too.
          <code>clearSigningKeyId()</code> restores automatic selection. <code>clearRecipientPublicKeyRings()</code> clears keys while keeping the
          cipher, so supply replacement keys before building. Algorithm reset methods restore the library defaults.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h2 id="received-conversion-details">Received-mail conversion details</h2>

      <p>A customer opens ticket 1042 to ask about an invoice. Their email has a short note, a logo, the invoice PDF and an earlier email attached.
        The ticket app can show the note and file names up front, then read the PDF when someone opens it.</p>

      <h3 id="streamed-text-bodies">Read the note and list the files</h3>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email preview = mail.converter()
    .fromEml(Path.of("ticket-1042.eml"))
    .withFetchAttachmentData(false)
    .toEmail();

String subject = preview.getSubject();
String note = preview.getPlainText();
List&lt;AttachmentResource&gt; files = preview.getAttachments();</code></pre>
      <p>The preview has the note and file metadata. <code>withFetchAttachmentData(false)</code> defers ordinary attachment payload reads.
        Body parsing, delivery reports and requested protection processing can still read selected content.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Stream ownership and text decoding</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>OpenPGP processing.</strong> For mail with a valid, ordinary non-OpenPGP MIME declaration, installing
            <code>openpgp-module</code> leaves unrelated attachments deferred.</li>
          <li><strong>Who closes each stream.</strong> Simple Java Mail closes the body, calendar and eager attachment streams it opens, even on failure.
            Your DataSource and any top-level EML stream you supply remain yours. Recoverable failures appear in <code>Email.getProblems()</code>
            with available source evidence. Conversion exceptions retain causes and suppressed failures. Cancellation and unrelated errors propagate.</li>
          <li><strong>How text is decoded.</strong> Already-decoded Strings keep the provider's interpretation. Text streams use the MIME charset and aliases.
            With no charset, the <code>us-ascii</code> default maps through Jakarta Mail's aliases to ISO-8859-1. Calendar streams honor their declared charset.
            An unsupported calendar charset can leave a Problem and the readable neighboring parts.</li>
          <li><strong>What metadata stays available.</strong> Eager and deferred extraction preserve the same Content-Type parameters, including
            <code>charset</code>. Parse out the base type if you need to test for <code>text/plain</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <h3 id="imported-inline-resources">Attachments and inline images</h3>
      <section aria-label="MIME structure and preview result">
        <div class="view">
          <h4>MIME in the ticket email</h4>
<pre><code class="language-text">multipart/mixed
├── multipart/alternative
│   ├── text/plain
│   └── multipart/related
│       ├── text/html
│       └── image/png (logo.png)
├── application/pdf (invoice.pdf)
└── message/rfc822 (earlier.eml)</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="side">
          <h4>What the preview gets</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Note.</strong> <code>getPlainText()</code> and <code>getHTMLText()</code> return the two versions of the note.</li>
            <li><strong>Embedded images.</strong> <code>getEmbeddedImages()</code> has <code>logo.png</code>, which the HTML references.</li>
            <li><strong>Attachments.</strong> <code>getAttachments()</code> has <code>invoice.pdf</code> and <code>earlier.eml</code> in source order.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>
      <p>When the handler needs the invoice, read just that resource. <code>readAllBytes()</code> closes the stream it opens.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">AttachmentResource invoice = files.stream()
    .filter(file -&gt; "invoice.pdf".equals(file.getName()))
    .findFirst()
    .orElseThrow();

byte[] invoiceBytes = invoice.readAllBytes();</code></pre>
      <p>The ticket has one file of that name. If names can repeat in your app, keep the attachment index from the preview.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>When a logo also appears as a download</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>A referenced image in <code>multipart/related</code> appears once in <code>getEmbeddedImages()</code> when it lacks
            <code>Content-Disposition</code>. An explicitly attached, referenced image has both an embedded and a downloadable view.</li>
          <li>Unreferenced inline resources with the same Content-ID remain separate attachments in source order, including any attachments between them.
            Eager and deferred imports classify them the same way and follow their chosen read timing.</li>
          <li>A decoded filename such as <code>&lt;draft&gt;.txt</code> or <code>UnknownAttachment</code> keeps its spelling. A missing filename can fall back
            to its CID. If both filename and Content-Type are absent, the fallback is <code>UnknownAttachment</code> and takes no payload read.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <h4 id="resource-representation-boundaries">Match Content-IDs</h4>
      <p>The ticket's HTML uses <code>src="cid:logo%2F1@example.org"</code>. Its image part has
        <code>Content-ID: &lt;logo/1@example.org&gt;</code>. Simple Java Mail matches those values during import. Look up the image by its
        Content-ID without the angle brackets.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Optional&lt;AttachmentResource&gt; logo = preview.getEmbeddedImages().stream()
    .filter(image -&gt; "logo/1@example.org".equals(image.getContentId()))
    .findFirst();</code></pre>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Content-ID matching rules and Outlook fallback</summary>
        <p>MIME/EML, MSG and replies built from readable Emails decode HTML character references once and then percent-decode CID URLs once.
          The scheme is case-insensitive and the ID is case-sensitive.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>src="cid:logo&amp;#64;example.org"</code> matches <code>Content-ID: &lt;logo@example.org&gt;</code>.
            <code>cid:logo@example.org.extra</code> refers to a different ID, and a literal <code>+</code> stays a plus.</li>
          <li><code>srcset="cid:logo&amp;#64;example.org 1x"</code> identifies that same logo. Each <code>srcset</code> URL is interpreted separately from
            its descriptor. Commas inside a URL stay part of that URL.</li>
          <li>MIME/EML import skips valid external comments and folding whitespace around the ID. For example,
            <code>Content-ID: &lt;logo@example.org&gt; (company logo)</code> matches <code>cid:logo@example.org</code>.</li>
          <li>Matching leaves original HTML, raw CSS, Content-IDs and image bytes alone. A filename such as <code>a&amp;amp;b.png</code> stays literal.
            The same <a href="/email-composition.html#automatic-image-character-references">HTML attribute interpretation</a> applies to automatic image lookup.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For MSG, Simple Java Mail checks the explicit ID, short filename and long filename in that order. Empty resources stay out of the image list.
          Use the converted Email for these shared matching rules. The raw Outlook source view shows the specialist reader's interpretation.</p>
      </details>

      <h3 id="conversion-body-parts">Body parts and encodings</h3>
      <p>The earlier email stays in <code>earlier.eml</code>, so the preview shows only the customer's new note. To see the MIME tree behind the
        preview, start with <code>preview.getSourceData().getParts()</code> and follow each part's <code>getChildren()</code>.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Body selection, transfer encodings and calendar parts</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Attached multipart content.</strong> Conversion keeps an explicitly attached child multipart together with its filename,
            Content-Type parameters and content.</li>
          <li><strong>What supplies the body.</strong> Ordinary nested body containers, including inline and filename-only multiparts, contribute text and
            resources. Repeated text and HTML parts follow the usual combination rules. The effective plain-text, HTML or calendar root supplies the body
            even if marked as an attachment. Use source inspection for the original MIME nesting.</li>
          <li id="empty-imported-bodies"><strong>Empty text stays empty.</strong> A readable empty body stays <code>""</code> in <code>getPlainText()</code> or
            <code>getHTMLText()</code> and in ordinary export. Defaults fill an absent body. Whitespace stays as received. Use inspection when
            an unavailable body and an absent body would lead to different decisions.</li>
          <li><strong>Transfer encoding can include comments.</strong> <code>Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64 (encoded for transport)</code> gives you
            decoded content and <code>ContentTransferEncoding.BASE_64</code>. Conflicting declarations, unsupported encodings and required read failures
            follow normal handling. Comments leave read timing and exact or protected bytes unchanged.</li>
          <li><strong>Decode the transport layer before opening protected mail.</strong> Base64 and quoted-printable around OpenPGP/MIME signatures or
            ciphertext are decoded for processing. The signed MIME entity and retained original EML stay intact. Give ordinary EML or MimeMessage conversion
            an <a href="security.html#section-reading-openpgp">OpenPGP receive configuration</a>. <code>withSmimeDecryption(...)</code> can also supply
            keys for independent S/MIME files inside that message. Their contents, originals and results stay with those
            <a href="security.html#signed-parent-attachments">protected attachments</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Use the calendar method from the MIME header first.</strong> Parameter names and recognized values are case-insensitive,
            including quoted parameters. For example, <code>request</code> becomes <code>CalendarMethod.REQUEST</code> while
            <code>getCalendarText()</code> preserves the text. Missing or unsupported methods follow normal calendar failure handling.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <h3 id="unparseable-imported-addresses">Addresses and headers</h3>
      <p>If the ticket has <code>Reply-To: broken &lt;address</code>, the preview can still show the sender's note and the address as received.
        Check the address before using it in an outgoing reply.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Address fallbacks, receipt headers and original evidence</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Malformed addresses.</strong> When MIME/EML address parsing fails, the offending From, To/Cc/Bcc or Reply-To text stays in
            its <code>Recipient</code> while conversion continues. Outgoing validation checks the retained value. Successful lenient parsing and
            missing-field fallbacks follow their usual rules.</li>
          <li><strong>Unusable bounce address.</strong> A MIME, EML or MSG message can still be imported. For
            <code>Return-Path: (comment only)</code>, <code>getBounceToRecipient()</code> returns null and <code>Email.getHeaders()</code> keeps the original
            header. Recipients you build yourself follow their normal lenient rules.</li>
          <li><strong>Parent and attached mail.</strong> Headers, bounce addresses and receipt destinations come from the effective root after protection
            processing. Import attached mail separately for its own headers. The attachment keeps its filename, type, description, ID and encoding.</li>
          <li><strong>Receipts and delivery reports.</strong> Case-insensitive <code>Disposition-Notification-To</code> and <code>Return-Receipt-To</code>
            supply ordered destinations and request flags, with usable values in the typed fields and header view. A converted notification exposes an
            immutable <code>DeliveryStatusReport</code>. Ordinary mail and mail with only an attached report return null for the parent's report.
            See <a href="/delivery-reports.html">Reading delivery reports</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Readable headers and original evidence.</strong> The case-insensitive header view agrees with typed fields where interpretation is
            available and can retain raw fallbacks. Source data keeps original occurrences and displaced alternatives, including evidence from other
            layers. Reconstructed output emits typed fields once, creates new MIME structure, keeps appropriate custom fields and drops obsolete delivery
            and authentication claims. Byte-preserving relay keeps the original representation. See
            <a href="security.html#section-reading-smime-merging">protected header selection</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <h3 id="interpreted-sent-dates">Sent dates</h3>
      <p>Ticket 1042 has an impossible sent date. Leave it out of the sent-time sort and show the original header in the support details.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">String rawDate = preview.getSourceData().getHeaders().stream()
    .filter(header -&gt; "Date".equalsIgnoreCase(header.getName()))
    .findFirst()
    .map(EmailSourceHeader::getRawValue)
    .orElse(null);

Instant sentAt = preview.getSentDateAsInstant();</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Raw Date header.</strong> <code>31 Feb 2026 10:20:30 +0000</code></li>
        <li><strong>Parsed sent date.</strong> <code>sentAt</code> is <code>null</code>. So is <code>getSentDate()</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Timezones, Outlook dates and export</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>MIME/EML.</strong> A missing or unknown timezone also leaves the parsed date null. Supported unambiguous formats remain usable.</li>
          <li><strong>Outlook.</strong> Use the actual client-submit time, then a usable Date header. A generic reader date can represent creation time
            and gives you no sent-date fallback. If both usable sources are missing, both Email sent-date getters return null. Missing source, creation
            or modification dates also leave the corresponding <code>OutlookMessageData</code> getter null. Returned Date objects are defensive copies.</li>
          <li><strong>Export.</strong> Ordinary reconstruction supplies a date if none is fixed. Exact EML keeps its original bytes.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <h3 id="undecodable-optional-headers">Recover optional metadata</h3>
      <p>A broken optional header can leave the note readable. Open <a href="#section-source-inspection">source inspection</a> to see what
        Simple Java Mail captured and check <code>getProblems()</code> for recoverable failures.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Header and attachment recovery</summary>
        <p>When a header's encoded-word charset is unavailable, conversion keeps the undecoded source text and unfolds legal folding. Known
          attachment or inline dispositions can survive malformed optional parameters using independent naming metadata or the unnamed fallback.
          Unknown or competing dispositions give you no ordinary attachment classification. Required content decoding, invalid options and
          unrelated application failures have their own error paths.</p>
      </details>
      <p>To send a reconstructed message, use <code>mailer.rehearse(email)</code> to prepare and validate it first. To relay the original bytes,
        use <a href="#section-exact-eml">exact EML</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h2 id="conversion-import-limits">Limit a received-mail import</h2>
      <p>Say your support inbox takes customer EML or MSG files. One upload can be too big, while another can hide a long chain of attached messages.
        Give each import a byte, part and depth allowance. Nested messages and opened protection views share that allowance.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ImportLimitsConfig limits = ImportLimitsConfig.builder()
    .withMaximumInputBytes(25L * 1024 * 1024)
    .withMaximumPartCount(1_000)
    .withMaximumNestingDepth(30)
    .build();

Email limited = mail.converter().fromEml(emlPath)
    .withImportLimitsConfig(limits)
    .toEmail();</code></pre>
      <p>Pick limits that fit your application. A missing choice inherits the factory setting, zero allows unlimited input for that choice,
        and a positive value sets the bound. Negative values are invalid. Reuse the same configuration with <code>toEmailBuilder()</code> if you need
        an editable result or <code>fromOutlookMsg(...)</code> for an Outlook file.</p>

      <h3 id="check-conversion-import-result">Check whether the import finished</h3>
      <p>If the importer reaches the part limit before reading a ticket attachment, the subject and body may still be usable. Check for
        <code>IMPORT_LIMIT_EXCEEDED</code> so the ticket can show a partial preview and hold any work that needs the full message.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">boolean hitLimit = limited.getProblems().stream()
    .anyMatch(problem -&gt; problem.getCode() == EmailProblem.Code.IMPORT_LIMIT_EXCEEDED);

if (hitLimit) {
    // Application code shows any available text and marks the preview incomplete.
    showPartialPreview(limited.getSubject(), limited.getPlainText());
}</code></pre>
      <p>Read any other Problems before trusting the result. See <a href="#section-source-inspection">source inspection</a> for originals,
        source links and historical Problems.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>How bytes, parts and depth are counted</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Input bytes.</strong> The bound counts the original encoded EML or MSG. String EML counts as UTF-8. Simple Java Mail checks
            while acquiring the input and may read one extra byte to confirm the limit was exceeded. An oversized input can leave an incomplete
            prefix and any available source evidence. Incomplete attachment bytes stay out of the normal attachment list.</li>
          <li><strong>Parts.</strong> The MIME root uses one part and interpreted children use more. An opened protection view uses a node at the same
            logical depth. Reading an admitted occurrence again for retention uses no additional part. For Outlook, root metadata uses the first node.
            Represented bodies, retained native bodies such as RTF and attachments are children. When the allowance runs out, later parts are omitted
            with bounded reporting.</li>
          <li><strong>Depth.</strong> Root depth is one. An embedded message root adds a level beneath its attachment. A depth stop omits that subtree
            and leaves eligible siblings available.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Original content and protection after a limit stop</summary>
        <p>Any complete raw input already acquired stays available even if traversal stops later. Losing a child part leaves an already read body and
          the original envelope structure in place. A retained protected attachment keeps its own protection facts.</p>
        <p>If a protection layer could not open, the available evidence and any permitted already read native fallback remain. Check the selected
          field's inspection for absence, unavailability, deliberately skipped inspection or unknown evidence. Use established inspection evidence
          for verification and decryption decisions.</p>
      </details>

      <h3 id="choose-conversion-import-source">Choose a source that can honor your limits</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Raw EML or Outlook MSG.</strong> Pass the original input to enforce a byte cap, a part count, a nesting depth, or all three.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplied <code>MimeMessage</code>.</strong> You can cap parts and depth. The original encoded size is unavailable, so a finite
          byte cap fails before the converter consumes the message. To clear that cap for one import, use <code>withMaximumInputBytes(0)</code>
          or <code>resetMaximumInputBytes()</code>, subject to locks.</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Supplied messages, direct conversions and CLI input</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Supplied <code>MimeMessage</code>.</strong> <code>getInspection().getOriginalMessage()</code> is null without captured raw input.
            A missing Date or Message-ID in an unsaved message stays missing when it is imported with limits, even if diagnostic serialization generates it.</li>
          <li><strong>Direct conversion.</strong> MIME/EML transformations that skip Email import reject explicit import limits. Combined
            Outlook-to-MIME/EML conversions apply the limits during import.</li>
          <li><strong>CLI message files.</strong> The CLI uses the request's captured factory. Copying and replying pass through a <code>MimeMessage</code>,
            so they reject a finite byte cap before reading the file. EML forwarding borrows MIME without the ordinary import defaults. MSG forwarding
            imports with its supported limits. See <a href="cli.html#message-file-import-limits">CLI message-file import limits</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Inheriting limits and budgeting memory</summary>
        <p>Inspect supplied choices before a read with <code>getImportLimitsConfig()</code>. Replacing the group replaces those choices.
          Use <code>useConfiguredImportLimitsConfig()</code> to restore all captured factory defaults, or <code>useConfiguredMaximumInputBytes()</code>
          to inherit just the byte setting. Parts and depth have corresponding reset and inheritance methods.
          See <a href="configuration.html#section-import-limits">property defaults and fixed values</a>.</p>
        <p>Budget memory and time for the whole operation. Input selection can copy an array or encode a String before import.
          Native parsers and crypto libraries can allocate separately. Deferred attachments are read later under their own ownership rules.
          Apply any acceptance limit for <a href="#section-exact-eml">exact EML relay</a> at its input.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h2 id="outlook-source-data" class="feature-heading">Outlook source data {{> components/module-badges outlook=true}}</h2>
      <p>When support receives a <code>.msg</code> and needs the raw headers, ask for the Email builder and Outlook data together.
        Use the Email for ordinary message fields and <code>OutlookMessageData</code> for the native message class and structural Outlook headers.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">OutlookEmailConversionResult result =
    mail.converter().fromOutlookMsg(msgFile).toEmailBuilderWithOutlookData();

Email email = result.buildEmail();
OutlookMessageData outlookData = result.getOutlookMessageData();

List&lt;String&gt; receivedHeaders = outlookData.getHeaderValues("Received");
String messageClass = outlookData.getMessageClass();
String rawHeaders = outlookData.getRawHeaders();
</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li id="outlook-receipt-header-names"><strong>Read receipt destinations in source order.</strong> Outlook recognizes
          <code>Disposition-Notification-To</code> and <code>Return-Receipt-To</code> regardless of name capitalization. Static and fluent imports,
          builder/data results and nested MSG imports expose the same typed lists. Repeated fields retain their order and duplicates even when they
          vary in capitalization. Other names keep their original spelling.</li>
        <li id="outlook-missing-attachment-type"><strong>Give an untyped file a download type.</strong> A readable attachment uses its supplied or
          filename-inferred type first, then <code>application/octet-stream</code>. A CID image whose type can't be inferred uses the same fallback.
          The name and content stay as received.</li>
        <li id="outlook-missing-sender"><strong>Supply a sender before forwarding.</strong> If the MSG lacks a sender address,
          <code>Email.getFromRecipient()</code> returns null. A sender name and native properties may still be in <code>Email.getSourceData()</code>.
          The last modifier stays separate. Copies preserve this evidence and the missing address. Set From before sending or opt the imported Email
          into Mailer defaults.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose which body to trust.</strong> With <code>email.getInspection().getHTMLText()</code> you can check the origin, derivation and
          protection of the HTML your app actually uses. Native HTML, HTML extracted from RTF and rendered RTF have distinct derivations.
          <code>outlookData.getBodyHTML()</code> and <code>getBodyRTF()</code> expose the native views separately. An invalid incoming signature can
          leave readable content and source-linked observations. See the <a href="security.html#section-inspecting-received-protection">received-protection workflow</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can import ordinary EML, MIME and Outlook mail without <code>smime-module</code>. Recognizable protected input and available metadata
        remain inspectable, with unsupported or unestablished facts unavailable or unknown. Inspection can still establish that plain content is unsigned
        and unencrypted. Add <code>smime-module</code> to request S/MIME decryption.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-exact-eml" id="section-exact-eml" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Submit finalized EML without rebuilding{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="exact-content"}}

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Say your billing service signs an invoice and saves it as <code>archive/invoice-2048.eml</code>. A delivery job picks it up later.
        <code>startingFromExactEml(...)</code> lets that job send the signed message straight from the archive and choose its SMTP envelope.</p>

      <h3>Send the archived invoice</h3>
      <p>Use a configured <code>Mailer</code> and give the builder at least one SMTP recipient. That address controls delivery even if the
        visible <code>To</code> or <code>Cc</code> says something else. Add an envelope sender if you want bounces sent to a specific mailbox.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
Path archiveFile = Path.of("archive", "invoice-2048.eml");

Email invoice = mail.emailBuilder()
    .startingFromExactEml(archiveFile)
    .withEnvelopeRecipients("ap@example.net")
    .withEnvelopeSender("bounces@example.org")
    .buildEmail();

MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(invoice);</code></pre>
      <p>Keep the receipt with the job so you can see what the relay accepted. See the <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-get-receipt">submission receipt guide</a>.</p>
      <p>Pass a <code>Path</code> and Simple Java Mail opens and closes the file for you. A supplied <code>InputStream</code> is read immediately,
        and your code closes it.</p>

      <h3>Check the send before it leaves</h3>
      <p>When you try the worker on a new route, run <code>rehearse</code> before <code>sendMail</code> to check the destination and compare the bytes
        with the archived file. A <a href="/debugging.html#section-email-validation">rehearsal</a> runs without an SMTP connection.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">MailRehearsal preview = mailer.rehearse(invoice);
if (!preview.getEnvelopeRecipients().equals(List.of("ap@example.net"))) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Unexpected delivery address");
}
if (!Arrays.equals(preview.getEmlBytes(), Files.readAllBytes(archiveFile))) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Rehearsal differs from the archived invoice");
}</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Check the file.</strong> Use complete, non-empty EML that Jakarta Mail can parse, with CRLF line endings and a final CRLF.
          The configured message size limit counts those bytes.</li>
        <li><strong>Review the headers.</strong> Every byte is preserved, including any <code>Bcc</code>, <code>Resent-Bcc</code> and
          <code>Content-Length</code> headers. If you need a Message-ID, set it before finalizing the file.</li>
        <li><strong>Finish the content before it reaches the worker.</strong> Apply templates, content checks, inline images and any outgoing
          DKIM, S/MIME or OpenPGP/MIME protection before archiving. Exact preparation bypasses the Mailer's ordinary defaults, overrides and
          content transformations. Mandatory locks still apply, and the Mailer rejects conflicts that would require rewriting the file.</li>
        <li><strong>Check your transport.</strong> The bundled Angus adapter preserves exact bytes. A replacement adapter must opt in.
          A <a href="/configuration.html#section-custom-mailer"><code>CustomMailer</code></a> receives the <code>Email</code> and a
          byte-preserving <code>MimeMessage</code>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Send the same bytes to a new mailbox</h3>
      <p>Accounts Payable moved to a new inbox and asks for another copy. Export the bytes from the exact <code>Email</code> and give them a new
        envelope. If you track delivery failures, you can tie the request to this send with an envelope ID.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">byte[] finalizedEml = mail.converter().fromEmail(invoice).toEmlBytes();
SmtpDeliveryOptions feedback = SmtpDeliveryOptions.builder()
    .withDeliveryStatusNotification(
        DeliveryStatusNotification.ReturnOption.HEADERS_ONLY,
        DeliveryStatusNotification.NotifyOption.FAILURE)
    .fixingEnvelopeId("invoice-2048-ap-move")
    .build();

Email redirected = mail.emailBuilder()
    .startingFromExactEml(finalizedEml)
    .withEnvelopeRecipients("ap-new@example.net")
    .withEnvelopeSender("bounces@example.org")
    .withSmtpDeliveryOptions(feedback)
    .buildEmail();

MailSubmissionReceipt redirectedReceipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(redirected);
String submittedEnvelopeId = redirectedReceipt.getEnvelopeId();</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>For a deliberate repeat to the same mailbox, send the original exact <code>invoice</code> again.</li>
        <li>When reusing a builder, check <code>getEnvelopeRecipients()</code>, <code>getEnvelopeSender()</code> and <code>getSmtpDeliveryOptions()</code>.
          Supply new recipients after <code>clearEnvelopeRecipients()</code>. <code>clearEnvelopeSender()</code> restores the provider's normal
          sender fallback.</li>
        <li>Save the receipt's envelope ID with the job. If a later report includes that ID, you can match it to this send.
          See <a href="/delivery-reports.html">reading delivery reports</a> and the envelope options for
          <a href="/email-composition.html#section-dsn-envelope-id">notifications and envelope IDs</a> or
          <a href="/security.html#section-requiretls">REQUIRETLS</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Read or edit a separate copy</h3>
      <p>You can read the original body, headers and attachments through the usual <code>Email</code> getters. The builder records
        <code>getInspection().getSmime()</code> and <code>getInspection().getOpenPgp()</code> facts with the available modules.
        Unestablished facts stay <code>UNKNOWN</code>, and the original bytes are ready for relay either way. See the
        <a href="/security.html#section-reading-smime-verification">S/MIME</a> and <a href="/security.html#section-reading-openpgp-status">OpenPGP</a>
        guides for the result meanings.</p>
      <p>Suppose support needs to read an S/MIME-encrypted invoice. With <code>smime-module</code> and the recipient's keys, open a separate
        view of the same file through <a href="#section-converting">ordinary conversion</a>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email readableInvoice = mail.converter()
    .fromEml(archiveFile)
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>To edit the message, start with <code>copying(invoice)</code>. An explicit call to
          <code>mailer.getEmailGovernance().produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(invoice)</code> also creates a composed
          <code>Email</code> from the parsed view, applying the Mailer's <a href="/configuration.html#section-config-mailer">defaults and overrides</a>.</li>
        <li>A rebuilt message can invalidate old signatures. Review any carried-over protection and apply fresh signing or encryption as needed.</li>
        <li>Keep the EML file for relay. A <a href="#section-serializing-emails">serialized Email</a> also needs the classes and optional modules
          stored in its object graph when restored.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Send the file from the command line</h3>
      <p>With your relay configured, run this from the archive directory. Relative paths use the calling client's working directory, even
        when a daemon handles the send. See the <a href="/cli.html">CLI guide</a>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm send --email:startingFromExactEml invoice-2048.eml --email:withEnvelopeRecipients ap@example.net</code></pre>
      <p>For a runnable Java example with relay setup and receipt output, see the
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/develop/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/ExactEmlSendDemoApp.java">exact EML demo</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-serializing-emails" id="section-serializing-emails" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Serializing Email objects{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Suppose a Java worker saves a composed order email to a queue and sends it after a restart. A supported Java snapshot can hold the Email
        and the settings it needs when sending. Here's a round trip using the composed message from the
        <a href="security.html#section-sending-openpgp">OpenPGP example</a>.</p>
<pre class="wide"><code class="language-java">byte[] snapshot;
try (ByteArrayOutputStream bytes = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
     ObjectOutputStream output = new ObjectOutputStream(bytes)) {
    output.writeObject(protectedEmail);
    snapshot = bytes.toByteArray();
}

Email restored;
try (ObjectInputStream input = new ObjectInputStream(
        new ByteArrayInputStream(snapshot))) {
    restored = (Email) input.readObject();
}

mailer.sync().sendMail(restored);</code></pre>
      <h3>Check what your queue stores</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Attachment bytes and MIME metadata.</strong> Serialization reads custom DataSources in full. Keep lazy or remote content available
          until the write completes. A read failure stops the write. A restored source is repeatable and read-only. Its original class and custom
          behavior, such as lazy loading, network access, caching or write support, stay with the original source.</li>
        <li><strong>Forwarded messages and recorded inspection.</strong> Forwarded MIME is stored in RFC 822 form and restored with a neutral Session.
          Supported snapshots retain complete originals, core inspection facts, unchanged occurrence associations, confidentiality requirements and
          draft permissions. Restoration uses those recorded facts without verifying the mail or recreating uncaptured evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Protection settings and secrets.</strong> S/MIME signing settings retain the PKCS12 data and passwords. OpenPGP retains signing key,
          hash and cipher choices. Signing carries the secret key ring and passphrase. Encryption-only settings keep the recipient public key rings.
          Treat the snapshot as sensitive because it can hold the full message, attachments, credentials and private keys.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens when the worker sends.</strong> A restored composed email applies its protection when sent. A restored
          <a href="#section-exact-eml">exact protected EML</a> retains its finished bytes. DKIM header exclusions stay detached and immutable.
          Build a replacement <code>DkimConfig</code> to change them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Give the worker the classes and optional modules stored in the snapshot's object graph. Before upgrading a queue that stores Email objects,
        check <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#serialized-email-compatibility">compatibility with older snapshots</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
